 matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix},$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}.$  Find the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}.$  Express your answer in the form \"$y = mx + b$\".","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"121135392e01aa03af7cf7c3784890ae79183a4897d6b6ca20c0f6a913d6a50a"},"seconds":71.86803579330444,"seed":167681709332735571,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under the transformation matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we first need to determine \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) itself. We know that \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) transforms the vectors \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's denote \\(\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\). Then, we have the following equations based on the given transformations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThese equations can be expanded into the following system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(2a - b = 9\\)\n2. \\(2c - d = 3\\)\n3. \\(a - 3b = 7\\)\n4. \\(c - 3d = -1\\)\n\nWe solve these equations step by step. First, we solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) from equations 1 and 3:\n\nFrom equation 1: \\(b = 2a - 9\\)\nSubstitute \\(b\\) into equation 3: \\(a - 3(2a - 9) = 7\\)\nThis simplifies to: \\(a - 6a + 27 = 7\\)\nSo, \\(-5a + 27 = 7\\)\nThus, \\(-5a = -20\\)\nTherefore, \\(a = 4\\)\nSubstitute \\(a = 4\\) back into \\(b = 2a - 9\\): \\(b = 2(4) - 9 = -1\\)\n\nSo, we have \\(a = 4\\) and \\(b = -1\\).\n\nNext, we solve for \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) from equations 2 and 4:\n\nFrom equation 2: \\(d = 2c - 3\\)\nSubstitute \\(d\\) into equation 4: \\(c - 3(2c - 3) = -1\\)\nThis simplifies to: \\(c - 6c + 9 = -1\\)\nSo, \\(-5c + 9 = -1\\)\nThus, \\(-5c = -10\\)\nTherefore, \\(c = 2\\)\nSubstitute \\(c = 2\\) back into \\(d = 2c - 3\\): \\(d = 2(2) - 3 = 1\\)\n\nSo, we have \\(c = 2\\) and \\(d = 1\\).\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nNow, we need to find the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under this transformation. A point on the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) can be written as \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Applying the transformation \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) to this point, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nLet the new coordinates be \\((X, Y)\\). Then, \\(X = 2x - 1\\) and \\(Y = 4x + 1\\). We need to express \\(Y\\) in terms of \\(X\\):\n\nFrom \\(X = 2x - 1\\), we solve for \\(x\\): \\(x = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\\)\nSubstitute this into \\(Y = 4x + 1\\): \\(Y = 4\\left(\\frac{X + 1}{2}\\right) + 1 = 2(X + 1) + 1 = 2X + 3\\)\n\nThus, the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under the transformation \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is \\(\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
